---
/**
 * Spam protection: honeypot, rate limits, Turnstile, AI score and the phishing guard.
 */
import CodeBlock from '../../ui/CodeBlock.astro';
import Callout from '../Callout.astro';
import DocsSection from './DocsSection.astro';
import { limits, fmt } from '../../../config/site';
import { FORM, sec } from './docsSections';

// Subheadings follow the section number, so adding a section never leaves them stale.
const n = sec('spam').no;

const honeypotCode = `
<!-- Real people never see it. Bots fill in every field they find. -->
<input type="text" name="_gotcha" style="display:none" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off">`;

const turnstileCode = `
<script src="https://challenges.cloudflare.com/turnstile/v0/api.js" async defer></script>

<form action="${FORM}" method="POST">
  <label>Your email <input name="email" type="email" required></label>
  <label>Message <textarea name="message" required></textarea></label>

  <!-- Adds a hidden cf-turnstile-response field once the check passes -->
  <div class="cf-turnstile" data-sitekey="0x4AAAAAAAyour-site-key"></div>

  <button>Send</button>
</form>`;
---

<DocsSection id="spam" kicker="Five layers, cheapest first">
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Spam protection is on for every form. Spam is still stored, in its own folder, so you can check nothing real was caught. It’s never delivered, and
      it’s deleted after {limits.spamRetentionDays} days.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3 class="ds-h3" id="spam-honeypot"><span class="mono ds-h3-no">{n}.1</span> Honeypot</h3>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      A field that’s hidden from people but visible to bots, which tend to fill in everything. Anything in it marks the submission as spam. Call it{' '}
      <code>_gotcha</code>, or pick your own name in <strong>Form settings → Spam</strong>.
    </p>
  </div>
  <CodeBlock code={honeypotCode} lang="html" filename="honeypot" compact class="wide" />

  <h3 class="ds-h3" id="spam-rate"><span class="mono ds-h3-no">{n}.2</span> Rate limits and heuristics</h3>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Each visitor can send {limits.ratePerIpPerMinute} submissions a minute to a form, and a form takes {fmt.n(limits.ratePerFormPerMinute)} a minute
      in total. Past that, they get a <code>429</code>. We count by a one-way hash of the IP address and never store the address itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then come the quick checks: a message stuffed with links, well-known spam phrases, throwaway email domains, a message that’s only a URL, and
      keyboard-mash gibberish.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3 class="ds-h3" id="spam-turnstile"><span class="mono ds-h3-no">{n}.3</span> Cloudflare Turnstile</h3>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Turnstile is Cloudflare’s free alternative to “click all the traffic lights”. There are two ways to use it, depending on how your form is sent.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="modes wide">
    <div class="mode">
      <p class="label mode-for">For plain HTML forms</p>
      <h4 class="h4">Challenge page</h4>
      <p>
        No setup. Turn it on in <strong>Form settings → Spam</strong>. When a submission looks borderline, the visitor is sent to a short check on{' '}
        <code>sendm8.com/c/…</code>. Once they pass, the submission goes through and they carry on to your thank-you page.
      </p>
      <a class="link-signal" href="/c/demo">See the challenge page</a>
    </div>
    <div class="mode">
      <p class="label mode-for">For AJAX forms</p>
      <h4 class="h4">Bring your own Turnstile</h4>
      <p>
        AJAX can’t follow a redirect to a challenge, so put the widget on your own form instead. We check the token it adds with your secret key.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="label steps-lead">Bring your own Turnstile, step by step</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      In the Cloudflare dashboard, open <strong>Turnstile</strong> and add a widget. Add your site’s hostname (for example <code>yourstudio.com</code>).
    </li>
    <li>Copy the <strong>site key</strong> into the widget on your form, as below.</li>
    <li>
      Paste the <strong>secret key</strong> into sendm8 under <strong>Form settings → Spam → Turnstile → Bring your own</strong>. It’s stored encrypted.
    </li>
    <li>
      That’s it. The widget adds a <code>cf-turnstile-response</code> field, and <code>FormData</code> picks it up automatically. Submissions without a
      valid token get a <code>403</code>.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <CodeBlock code={turnstileCode} lang="html" filename="contact.html" highlight={[1, 8]} class="wide" />

  <h3 class="ds-h3" id="spam-ai"><span class="mono ds-h3-no">{n}.4</span> AI spam score</h3>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Optional, and off until you switch it on in <strong>Form settings → Spam</strong>. A small model scores each message after it’s been stored, so it
      never slows your visitor down. Anything it’s confident about moves to spam before it’s delivered. You’ll see the score and the reasons on every
      submission.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3 class="ds-h3" id="spam-phishing"><span class="mono ds-h3-no">{n}.5</span> Phishing guard</h3>
  <Callout title="Forms that ask for passwords are held" kind="signal" icon="shield">
    <p>
      Free form backends get used to collect stolen logins. If a form has fields named like <code>password</code>, <code>card_number</code>,{' '}
      <code>cvv</code>, <code>ssn</code> or <code>seed phrase</code>, its submissions are held instead of delivered, and the form is flagged for review.
      If that’s a false alarm (say, a “forgot password” support form), the dashboard explains what to do.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</DocsSection>

<style>
  .wide {
    max-width: 100%;
  }

  /* Turnstile modes */
  .modes {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    border: 2px solid var(--line-strong);
  }
  .mode {
    display: grid;
    align-content: start;
    gap: var(--s-2);
    padding: var(--s-5);
    font-size: var(--fs-sm);
  }
  .mode + .mode {
    border-left: 2px dashed var(--line);
  }
  .mode p:not(.label) {
    color: var(--fg-muted);
  }
  .mode-for {
    color: var(--signal-text);
  }
  .mode a {
    font-size: var(--fs-sm);
  }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .modes {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
    .mode + .mode {
      border-left: 0;
      border-top: 2px dashed var(--line);
    }
  }
</style>
